From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sr-0w <Sr-0w@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 01:45:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: camss: give sm8150 its own CSIPHY generation

SM8150 was given CAMSS_845 because sdm845 was the closest supported part, and
that is right for the CSID and VFE, which are the same generation. It is wrong
for the CSIPHY.

The published vendor kernel says so directly: its sm8150 camera device tree
declares every CSIPHY as "qcom,csiphy-v1.1", while sdm845 is v1.0. The two
sequences differ from the first common-block register onwards, and the vendor's
own headers carry 440 register writes for v1.1 against 272 for v1.0.

Mainline already has the v1.1 sequence, as lane_regs_sc8280xp, and it matches
the vendor's v1.1 table entry for entry once padding is discounted. So this
adds CAMSS_8150, which behaves as CAMSS_845 everywhere except the CSIPHY lane
table, where it takes the v1.1 one.

Symptom this fixes: the sensor transmits and the CSID is correctly configured
for four lanes over D-PHY, but latches receive errors and never interrupts,
because the physical layer was set up for the wrong generation.

Signed-off-by: Sr-0w <Sr-0w@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csiphy-3ph-1-0.c | 14 ++++++++
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe.c            |  2 +
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.c                |  2 +-
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.h                |  1 +
 4 files changed, 18 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.h
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.h
@@ -85,6 +85,7 @@
 	CAMSS_8250,
 	CAMSS_8280XP,
 	CAMSS_845,
+	CAMSS_8150,
 	CAMSS_8550,
 	CAMSS_X1E80100,
 };
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csiphy-3ph-1-0.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csiphy-3ph-1-0.c
@@ -748,6 +748,7 @@
 	case CAMSS_8250:
 	case CAMSS_8280XP:
 	case CAMSS_845:
+	case CAMSS_8150:
 	case CAMSS_8550:
 	case CAMSS_X1E80100:
 		ret = true;
@@ -829,6 +830,16 @@
 		regs->lane_regs = &lane_regs_sdm845[0];
 		regs->lane_array_size = ARRAY_SIZE(lane_regs_sdm845);
 		break;
+	/*
+	 * SM8150 carries a v1.1 PHY, which the published vendor kernel states
+	 * outright and whose register table matches sc8280xp's entry for entry.
+	 * Driving it with sdm845's v1.0 sequence leaves the receiver latching
+	 * errors and decoding nothing.
+	 */
+	case CAMSS_8150:
+		regs->lane_regs = &lane_regs_sc8280xp[0];
+		regs->lane_array_size = ARRAY_SIZE(lane_regs_sc8280xp);
+		break;
 	case CAMSS_7280:
 	case CAMSS_8250:
 		regs->lane_regs = &lane_regs_sm8250[0];
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe.c
@@ -345,6 +345,7 @@
 	case CAMSS_8250:
 	case CAMSS_8280XP:
 	case CAMSS_845:
+	case CAMSS_8150:
 	case CAMSS_8550:
 	case CAMSS_X1E80100:
 		switch (sink_code) {
@@ -1973,6 +1974,7 @@
 	case CAMSS_8250:
 	case CAMSS_8280XP:
 	case CAMSS_845:
+	case CAMSS_8150:
 	case CAMSS_8550:
 	case CAMSS_X1E80100:
 		ret = 16;
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.c
@@ -3993,7 +3993,7 @@
 };
 
 static const struct camss_resources sm8150_resources = {
-	.version = CAMSS_845,
+	.version = CAMSS_8150,
 	.pd_name = "top",
 	.csiphy_res = csiphy_res_8150,
 	.csid_res = csid_res_8150,
--
2.50.1
